window.DECK = {"title": "Who receives the gains from automation?", "date": "November 25, 2026", "duration": 110, "description": "Moll–Rachel–Restrepo on wages, returns and wealth; Restrepo on work in an AGI economy; Jones–Tonetti on weak links and long-run factor shares; quantitative inequality evidence.", "draft_date": "2026-10-25", "author": "Sergio Ocampo", "slides": [{"title": "Who receives the gains from automation?", "html": "<p>Automation, asset ownership and the labour share<\/p><p>Sergio Ocampo<br>ECON 3399 · AI and Robots at Work<\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 0.5, "source": "ECON 3399 · Course explanation or explicitly labelled classroom illustration.", "class": "cover", "id": "s01", "teaching_role": "setup"}, {"id": "longrun-roadmap", "title": "Wages, income shares and ownership", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:48%\"><col style=\"width:52%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Paper<\/th><th>Question<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<br><em>Uneven Growth<\/em><\/td><td>Who receives the gains when automation raises capital demand?<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Restrepo (2025)<br><em>We Won’t be Missed<\/em><\/td><td>What if all valuable work can be performed with compute?<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "paper-moll", "title": "Uneven Growth", "html": "<div class=\"paper-identity-layout\"><figure><img src=\"figures/paper_moll.png\" alt=\"Published article · First page: Uneven Growth\"><figcaption>Published article · First page<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"paper-details\"><p class=\"paper-subtitle\">Automation’s impact<br>on income and wealth inequality<\/p><p>Benjamin Moll, Łukasz Rachel, and Pascual Restrepo<\/p><p><strong>Econometrica · 2022<\/strong><\/p><p class=\"small\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the paper<\/a><\/p><\/div><\/div>", "notes": "", "minutes": 0, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the paper<\/a> · Econometrica · 2022", "class": "paper-identity content-centered", "teaching_role": "paper_identity"}, {"title": "Can capital accumulation make growth broadly shared?", "html": "<p><strong>Approach:<\/strong> A task model with unequal skills and asset holdings.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Households save and accumulate wealth<\/li><li>Returns to savings/wealth determined by productivity of capital<\/li><li>Solve for wages, returns and the distribution of income<\/li><li>Calibrate an automation experiment to U.S. data<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">The new channel: higher capital returns change wealth accumulation.<\/div>", "notes": "", "minutes": 4, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022), Uneven Growth<\/a>", "id": "s03", "revision": "results-focus-2026-10-26", "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"title": "Two sources of income", "html": "<p>Households differ in skill \\(z\\); labour earnings are \\(w_z\\).<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[y_j=w_z+r a_j\\]<\/div>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:27%\"><col style=\"width:73%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(y_j\\)<\/td><td>Annual income of household \\(j\\)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(w_z\\)<\/td><td>Annual labour earnings for skill group \\(z\\)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(a_j\\)<\/td><td>Assets owned: a stock<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(r=R-\\delta\\)<\/td><td>Net annual return: gross rental \\(R\\) minus depreciation \\(\\delta\\)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">Competitive wages and capital rentals equal marginal products.<br>Household saving determines the supply of capital.<\/div>", "notes": "", "minutes": 4, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022), Uneven Growth<\/a>", "id": "s04", "teaching_role": "setup", "revision": "definitions-2026-10-29", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-production-setup", "title": "Tasks, automation and aggregate production", "html": "<ul><li>Each skill group \\(z\\) performs a different set of tasks.<\/li><li>Capital takes over a fraction \\(\\alpha_z\\) of that group’s tasks.<\/li><\/ul>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:25%\"><col style=\"width:75%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(\\eta_z\\), \\(\\sum_z\\eta_z=1\\)<\/td><td>Value-added weight of group \\(z\\)’s tasks<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\ell_z\\)<\/td><td>Group population share<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\psi_z\\)<\/td><td>Labour productivity at the group’s tasks<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(Y\\), \\(K\\), \\(A\\)<\/td><td>Output; capital stock; productivity, including automation’s cost savings<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[Y=A K^\\alpha\\prod_z(\\psi_z\\ell_z)^{(1-\\alpha_z)\\eta_z},\\qquad \\alpha=\\sum_z\\eta_z\\alpha_z\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Cobb–Douglas task aggregation: automation raises capital’s share and lowers labour’s.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equation (2); Assumption 1", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-factor-payments", "title": "Competitive factor payments and income shares", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\Pi=\\max_{K^d,\\{n_z\\}}\\left\\{Y-RK^d-\\sum_z w_z n_z\\right\\}\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad Y=A(K^d)^\\alpha\\prod_z(\\psi_z n_z)^{(1-\\alpha_z)\\eta_z}\\]<\/div>\n<p><strong>Optimality Conditions<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[RK=\\alpha Y,\\qquad w_z\\ell_z=(1-\\alpha_z)\\eta_zY\\]<\/div>\n<p>Market clearing: \\(K^d=K\\), \\(n_z=\\ell_z\\).<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\text{Net Return: }r=R-\\delta;\\qquad\\qquad\\text{Wage Bill: }\\bar w=\\sum_z w_z\\ell_z=(1-\\alpha)Y\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Full adoption: \\(R&lt;w_z/\\psi_z\\) → use capital wherever automation is feasible.<br>Automation changes task shares; equilibrium output determines income levels.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Section 2.1; Definition 1", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "s08", "title": "Consumption, saving and wealth resets", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\max_{\\{c_t\\}}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\rho t}u(c_t)\\,dt,\\qquad u'(c)=c^{-\\sigma}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation equation-fragments\" style=\"margin-top:-16px\">\\(\\displaystyle\\dot a_t=r a_t+w_z-c_t,\\qquad a_t\\ge-\\frac{w_z}{r}\\)<span class=\"muted-math\" style=\"color:var(--muted);margin-left:48px\">\\(\\displaystyle\\left(\\dot y=\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)\\)<\/span><\/div>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:22%\"><col style=\"width:78%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Parameter<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(\\rho=\\varrho+p\\)<\/td><td>Effective discount rate; \\(\\varrho\\): pure time preference<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\sigma\\)<\/td><td>Inverse intertemporal elasticity of substitution<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(p\\)<\/td><td>Annual reset hazard: assets return to 0; skill is retained<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<p class=\"discrete-equivalent\" style=\"color:var(--muted)\">Discrete Time Equivalent:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation muted-equation\" style=\"color:var(--muted);margin-top:-20px\">\\[\\max_{\\{c_t\\}}\\sum_{t=0}^\\infty\\beta^t u(c_t),\\qquad\\beta=e^{-\\rho},\\qquad a_{t+1}=(1+r)a_t+w_z-c_t\\]<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equation (1)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-saving-rule", "title": "The optimal saving rule", "html": "<p>At constant wages and returns, define <strong>effective wealth<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[x_t=a_t+\\frac{w_z}{r}\\]<\/div>\n<p>Financial assets + the present value of future labour income.<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\dot a_t=\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}x_t,\\qquad c_t=\\left(r-\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\right)x_t,\\qquad \\frac{\\dot x_t}{x_t}=\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">A higher return raises the rate of accumulation; resets return \\(x_t\\) to \\(w_z/r\\).<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#s09\">Euler equation and the saving-rule derivation<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Lemma 1; equation (3)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-capital-market-equations", "title": "The equations behind capital supply and demand", "html": "<p>Aggregate the saving rule; subtract assets consumed at resets:<\/p>\n<div class=\"columns steady-state-row\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1.65fr 1.05fr 1.30fr;gap:14px;align-items:center\">\n<div><div class=\"equation\">\\[\\dot K=\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\left(K+\\frac{\\bar w}{r}\\right)-pK\\]<\/div><\/div>\n<div><div class=\"equation purple-arrow\" style=\"color:var(--purple)\">\\[\\xrightarrow{\\text{Steady State }\\dot K=0}\\]<\/div><\/div>\n<div><div class=\"equation\">\\[K=\\frac{1-\\rho/r}{p\\sigma+\\rho-r}\\,\\bar w\\]<\/div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>Normalize capital by wage bill: \\(k=K/\\bar w\\).<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation purple-underbrace-labels\">\\[\\underbrace{k^s(r)=\\frac{1-\\rho/r}{p\\sigma+\\rho-r}}_{\\text{household saving}},\\qquad\\underbrace{k^d(r)=\\frac{\\alpha}{(1-\\alpha)(r+\\delta)}}_{\\text{firm factor demand}}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">The market clears where \\(k^s(r^*)=k^d(r^*)\\).<br>Higher \\(\\alpha\\) shifts demand; the supply parameters stay fixed.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equations (4), (6) and (7)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "s11", "title": "Capital demand meets long-run capital supply", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/moll_capital_market_clear.png\" style=\"max-height:330px\" alt=\"Illustrative equilibrium of capital supply and demand. Dashed line: no-reset benchmark r equals rho.\"><\/figure>\n<div class=\"takeaway\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\(\\displaystyle r^*=\\rho+p\\sigma\\underbrace{\\frac{r^*K^*}{r^*K^*+\\bar w^*}}_{\\text{Capital Share}},\\qquad \\frac{K^*}{\\bar w^*}=\\frac{\\alpha}{(1-\\alpha)(r^*+\\delta)}\\).<\/div>\n<p><a href=\"#moll-no-reset\" class=\"action\">No-reset benchmark<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Section 2.2 / Figure 1; teaching redraw", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-return-equilibrium", "title": "The equilibrium return and the net capital share", "html": "\n<p>Define the capital share <strong>after replacing depreciated capital<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\alpha_{\\rm net}=\\frac{rK}{rK+\\bar w}=\\frac{rK}{Y-\\delta K}\\]<\/div>\n<p>Capital-market clearing implies:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\underbrace{r^*-\\rho}_{\\text{return gap}}=p\\sigma\\alpha_{\\rm net}^*,\\qquad \\rho&lt;r^*&lt;\\rho+p\\sigma\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Automation raises \\(\\alpha_{\\rm net}^*\\) → a permanently higher return.<br>Without wealth resets, \\(p=0\\) → the benchmark return is \\(r^*=\\rho\\).<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">\\(\\alpha\\): gross capital share; \\(\\alpha_{\\rm net}\\): net share; *: steady state. <a href=\"#s13\">Derivation<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Proposition 1; equation (5)", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "s12", "title": "Automation raises the long-run return to wealth", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/moll_automation_shift_clear.png\" style=\"max-height:330px\" alt=\"Illustration: \\(\\alpha\\): 35% → 43%; household saving parameters stay fixed.\"><figcaption>Illustration: \\(\\alpha\\): 35% → 43%; household saving parameters stay fixed.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Capital demand shifts out → more capital and a higher net return.<br>In the no-reset benchmark, capital expands until the return returns to impatience.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-transition\" class=\"action\">Transition paths<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 2; teaching redraw", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-output-equation", "title": "Higher returns limit the output expansion", "html": "<div class=\"equation\" style=\"padding:0\">\\[\\frac{K^*}{Y^*}=\\frac{\\alpha}{R^*}\\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad d\\ln(K/Y)^*=\\frac{d\\alpha}{\\alpha}-d\\ln R^*\\]<\/div>\n<p>Higher \\(R^*\\) limits <em>capital deepening<\/em>: the expansion of \\(K/Y\\).<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\" style=\"padding:0\">\\[d\\ln Y^*=\\underbrace{d\\ln\\mathrm{TFP}_{\\alpha}}_{\\sum_z\\eta_z\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{w_z^*}{\\psi_zR^*}\\right)d\\alpha_z>0}+\\alpha\\,d\\ln K^*+\\underbrace{\\sum_z(1-\\alpha_z)\\eta_z\\,d\\ln n_z}_{0:\\ \\text{fixed labour supply}}\\]<\/div>\n<p>Use \\(d\\ln K^*=d\\ln(K/Y)^*+d\\ln Y^*\\):<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation purple-underbrace-labels\" style=\"padding:0\">\\[d\\ln Y^*=\\underbrace{\\frac{d\\ln\\mathrm{TFP}_{\\alpha}}{1-\\alpha}}_{\\text{efficiency gain}}+\\underbrace{\\frac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\,d\\ln(K/Y)^*}_{\\text{capital deepening}}=\\frac{d\\ln\\mathrm{TFP}_{\\alpha}+d\\alpha-\\alpha\\,d\\ln R^*}{1-\\alpha}\\]<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equations (2), (9); exact log differentiation", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-wage-equation", "title": "Output gains versus displacement in wages", "html": "\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\bar w=(1-\\alpha)Y,\\qquad w_z=\\frac{(1-\\alpha_z)\\eta_z}{\\ell_z}Y\\]<\/div>\n<p>With skill supplies and task weights fixed:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[d\\ln w_z=\\underbrace{d\\ln Y}_{\\text{output gain}}-\\underbrace{\\frac{d\\alpha_z}{1-\\alpha_z}}_{\\text{task displacement}}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Higher returns limit the expansion of capital and output.<br>The output gain may then be too small to offset displacement.<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#moll-wage-algebra\">Productivity, returns and the wage equation<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Proposition 2; equation (10)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-inequality-channels", "title": "Automation increases inequality", "html": "<div>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:26px;color:var(--purple);font-weight:500;margin:0 0 12px\">Direct effects on labor income<\/h2>\n<ul style=\"margin:0\"><li>Skill groups differ in their exposure to task automation.<\/li><li>More-exposed groups suffer larger relative wage losses; wage levels can also fall.<\/li><\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<div>\n<h2 style=\"font-size:26px;color:var(--purple);font-weight:500;margin:0 0 12px\">Indirect effect through asset returns<\/h2>\n<ul style=\"margin:0\"><li>More assets → larger income gains when returns rise.<\/li><li><a href=\"#moll-tail-mechanism\">Higher returns increase wealth inequality<\/a> through faster accumulation.<\/li><li>With capital returns \\(r_K\\) and safe-bond returns \\(r_B\\), automation widens \\(r_K-r_B\\).<\/li><li>Capital investors gain more than bond holders → asset composition also matters.<\/li><\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> Simulate the full model with <a href=\"#moll-portfolio-return\">risky capital, bonds and portfolio returns<\/a>.<\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Propositions 2–5; Sections 2–3", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "s21", "title": "The experiment targets routine tasks and the capital share", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/moll_automation_by_percentile.png\" style=\"max-height:285px\" alt=\"Figure 7. Horizontal: 1980 wage percentile. Vertical: calibrated fraction of tasks automated.\"><figcaption>Figure 7. Horizontal: 1980 wage percentile. Vertical: calibrated fraction of tasks automated.<\/figcaption><\/figure><ul><li>Gross capital share: \\(\\alpha=\\sum_z\\eta_z\\alpha_z\\): 34.5% → 42.8%<\/li><li>Routine-task exposure allocates automation across skill groups<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"takeaway\">The capital-share change is a calibration input.<br>Wages, asset returns and income inequality are model outcomes.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 7 and Table I", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-aggregates", "title": "Capital returns rise more than bond returns", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/results_focus_returns.png\" style=\"max-height:350px\" alt=\"Figure 8: each point is a long-run model economy, indexed by the gross capital share.\"><figcaption>Figure 8: each point is a long-run model economy, indexed by the gross capital share.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Returns to productive capital rise much more than safe-bond returns.<br>The capital–bond return gap widens as automation increases.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 8, PDF p. 28", "minutes": 4.5, "revision": "results-focus-2026-10-26", "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-output-wages", "title": "Higher output per hour does not lift the median wage", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:335px\" src=\"figures/results_focus_output_wages.png\" alt=\"Figure 8 · model steady states; dollars per hour. Mean wage: \\((1-\\alpha)Y/\\varphi\\), with fixed markup \\(\\varphi\\).\"><figcaption>Figure 8 · model steady states; dollars per hour. Mean wage: \\((1-\\alpha)Y/\\varphi\\), with fixed markup \\(\\varphi\\).<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Output per hour rises, the mean wage is roughly flat, and the median wage falls.<br>Aggregate productivity growth does not describe the typical worker’s gain.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-capital-tfp\" class=\"action\">Capital intensity and efficiency gains<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 8, PDF p. 28", "minutes": 4.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "revision": "results-focus-2026-10-26", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-wages", "title": "The wage effects differ across the distribution", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:350px\" src=\"figures/moll_wages.png\" alt=\"Figure 9 · predicted change (left); data, 1980–2012/16 (right). With fixed markup: \\(w_z\\propto(1-\\alpha_z)Y\\).\"><figcaption>Figure 9 · predicted change (left); data, 1980–2012/16 (right). With fixed markup: \\(w_z\\propto(1-\\alpha_z)Y\\).<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Model wages fall below roughly the 80th percentile.<br>About −10% near the 25th percentile; +7% near the 95th.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 9 and Section 5.1, PDF p. 29", "minutes": 6.5, "revision": "results-2026-10-25", "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"title": "Unequal income growth: Comparing steady states", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:350px\" src=\"figures/income_growth.png\" alt=\"Figure 10: model income changes by total-income percentile; shaded contributions from labour and capital.\"><figcaption>Figure 10: model income changes by total-income percentile; shaded contributions from labour and capital.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Bottom-half incomes fall about 5–10%; incomes around the 50th–80th percentiles stagnate; the top 0.1% gains about 55%.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#moll-tail\">Higher returns and upper-tail inequality<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 7.0, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022), Uneven Growth<\/a> · Figure 10, PDF p. 30", "id": "s22", "revision": "results-2026-10-25", "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-data", "title": "The data also show sharply uneven income growth", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:350px\" src=\"figures/moll_income_data.png\" alt=\"Figure 12: U.S. income growth by percentile, 1980–2012; IRS fiscal income and DINA national-income concepts.\"><figcaption>Figure 12: U.S. income growth by percentile, 1980–2012; IRS fiscal income and DINA national-income concepts.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Both sources show much slower growth below the median than at the top.<br>The top 0.1% grows around 5% per year.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 12, PDF p. 33", "minutes": 7.0, "revision": "results-2026-10-25", "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "paper-restrepo", "title": "We Won’t be Missed", "html": "<div class=\"paper-identity-layout\"><figure><img src=\"figures/paper_restrepo_agi.png\" alt=\"Restrepo, October 22 2025, first page\"><figcaption>Chapter · First page<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"paper-details\"><p class=\"paper-subtitle\">Work and Growth<br>in the AGI World<\/p><p>Pascual Restrepo<\/p><p>NBER conference volume chapter<br>October 22, 2025<\/p><p><a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open the paper<\/a><\/p><\/div><\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 0.5, "teaching_role": "paper_identity", "class": "paper-identity content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-premises", "title": "AGI is a recipe; compute is a scarce resource", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:27%\"><col style=\"width:73%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Premise<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>AGI<\/td><td>Every task becomes feasible with compute; its compute requirement converges to a finite, positive value<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Compute capacity, \\(K_t\\)<\/td><td>Computations available per unit of time; finite today, unbounded over time<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Human skills<\/td><td>Fixed supplies and accessible task sets for each skill<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Production & markets<\/td><td>Constant returns to scale; efficient allocation; factors paid marginal products<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">Feasible automation need not be economical immediately.<br>The model asks how scarce compute should be allocated.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-task-production", "title": "A task can use human time or compute", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[y_t(i)=\\ell_t(i)+\\frac{k_t(i)}{\\gamma_t(i)}\\]<\/div><table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:33%\"><col style=\"width:67%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Symbol<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(i\\), \\(y_t(i)\\)<\/td><td>Task index; quantity completed in human-work units<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\ell_t(i)\\), \\(k_t(i)\\)<\/td><td>Human input; compute allocated to that work<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\gamma_t(i)\\) <span class=\"muted-math\" style=\"color:var(--muted)\">\\(\\left(\\frac{a_\\ell(i)}{a_k(i)}\\right)\\)<\/span><\/td><td>Compute needed to replace one unit of human work<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p><strong>Firms allocate labour and compute across tasks.<\/strong> Total compute and each skill’s available human time are limited.<\/p><div class=\"takeaway\">Human productivity is normalized to one in the task equation.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-bottleneck-definition", "title": "Bottleneck and supplementary work", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[Y=\\mathcal{F}\\big(y(i)\\big)\\]<\/div>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:25%\"><col style=\"width:75%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Type of work<\/th><th>Meaning for sustained growth<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Bottleneck<\/td><td>Must expand—or become arbitrarily valuable—as output expands<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><\/td><td>Potential examples: energy, logistics and science<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Supplementary<\/td><td>Can stay fixed, with bounded value of an extra unit, while output grows<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><\/td><td>Potential examples: arts, hospitality and some customer support<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<p>The distinction depends on substitutability across tasks in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\):<br>how easily more output from other tasks can compensate for limited output of one task.<\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-bottleneck-figure", "title": "Production determines which tasks are bottlenecks", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[Y_t=\\mathcal{F}\\big(\\{y_t(i)\\}_i\\big)\\]<\/div>\n<figure><img src=\"figures/agi_bottleneck_supplementary.png\" style=\"max-height:300px\" alt=\"Illustrative aggregate technologies. Left: with task2 fixed at1, extra task1 output approaches a ceiling of2. Right: supplementary work stays at1 while core output and aggregate output can keep growing.\"><figcaption>Teaching examples: \\(y_2=1\\) (left), \\(y_s=1\\) (right); \\(y_b\\): core task output.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">A fixed bottleneck can cap output or develop an unbounded scarcity value.<br>Supplementary work can stay fixed without either outcome.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · Definitions 1–2; illustrative aggregate technologies", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-automation-choice", "title": "Which tasks eventually use compute?", "html": "<p>AGI makes every task feasible at finite compute cost; total compute keeps expanding.<\/p>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:23%\"><col style=\"width:40%\"><col style=\"width:37%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Human-only task<\/th><th>What happens as the economy grows?<\/th><th>Why allocate compute?<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Bottleneck<\/td><td>Its fixed supply caps output, or its scarcity value keeps rising<\/td><td>Expanding this task eventually becomes worth the compute<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Supplementary<\/td><td>Its quantity and marginal value can both stay bounded<\/td><td>Its value may stay too low to divert compute from other work<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">All bottlenecks eventually use compute, possibly alongside people.<br>Some supplementary work can remain human-only.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · Definitions 1–2; Proposition 1", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-labour-share", "title": "Positive wages can coexist with a vanishing labour share", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/agi_income_and_share.png\" style=\"max-height:325px\" alt=\"Labour share = total labour income / output. Illustration: wage bill = 1.25; output = (K + 10) / 8.\"><figcaption>Labour share = total labour income / output. Illustration: wage bill = 1.25; output = (K + 10) / 8.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Compute K: 10 → 190; labour’s share: 50% → 5%.<br>The wage bill stays positive while income paid to compute expands.<\/div><p><a href=\"#agi-worked-example\" class=\"action\">Two-task example<\/a> · <a href=\"#agi-wage-bill\" class=\"action\">Aggregate wage-bill comparison<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "agi-weak-links", "title": "Full automation gives the same limiting factor shares", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:23%\"><col style=\"width:38.5%\"><col style=\"width:38.5%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th><\/th><th>Restrepo<\/th><th>Jones–Tonetti: full automation<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Assumption<\/td><td>Every task has finite compute cost \\(\\gamma(i)\\); total compute keeps growing<\/td><td>Machines become cheaper on all tasks; 100% automation in finite time<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Consequence<\/td><td>Compute can expand every bottleneck<\/td><td>Capital can expand every essential task<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<ul><li>No essential task must rely only on fixed human input<\/li><li>Human wages are limited by replication costs; output keeps expanding<\/li><\/ul>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\frac{\\text{Labour income}}{Y}\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad\\frac{\\text{Compute / capital income}}{Y}\\longrightarrow1\\]<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · Premises 1–2, Propositions 1–5; <a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026), May v0.5<\/a> · Sections 5.2–5.3, Figure 5", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "jt-human-bottlenecks", "title": "Jones–Tonetti: when human weak links remain", "html": "<p>Low substitution across tasks: \\(\\sigma&lt;1\\).<br>Extra machine-task output cannot fully replace scarce human-task output.<\/p>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:26%\"><col style=\"width:51%\"><col style=\"width:23%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Automation assumption<\/th><th>Why does human work stay scarce?<\/th><th>Capital-share limit<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><span style=\"color:#75a826\">Permanent<br>human tasks<\/span><\/td><td>A set of essential tasks can never use machines; adding machines cannot expand this work<\/td><td><span style=\"color:#75a826\">0%<\/span><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><span style=\"color:#006fb9\">Ever harder<br>last tasks<\/span><\/td><td>Replication costs rise without bound near the last task; the human-task set shrinks but never disappears<\/td><td><span style=\"color:#006fb9\">≈36%<\/span><br>in their baseline calibration<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Permanent or increasingly hard human tasks preserve human scarcity.<br>These are different automation limits from Restrepo’s finite-cost AGI premise.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026), May v0.5<\/a> · Sections 5.2–5.3, Figures 4–5", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "jt-weak-links", "title": "Weak links can preserve the value of human work", "html": "<p>Jones & Tonetti: output needs both calculation and client judgment.<br><strong>Weak links:<\/strong> tasks whose limited output constrains the whole process.<\/p>\n<figure><img src=\"figures/jt_weak_link_example.png\" style=\"max-height:250px\" alt=\"Illustrative two-task CES technology. With human task output fixed at one, increasing machine task output from one to eight raises final output toward 1.19, while labour's income share rises from 50 percent toward 100 percent.\"><figcaption>Illustration: human task output stays at 1; labour share = wage bill / output. <a href=\"#jt-setup\">Paper + setup<\/a> · <a href=\"#agi-weak-links\">Bottleneck comparison<\/a><\/figcaption><\/figure>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Better machines on a fixed set of tasks can raise labour’s income share.<br>Automation sustains growth by bringing rapidly improving machines to more tasks.<br><a href=\"#jt-historical\">Better machines needed to materialize growth<\/a><\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "jt-future", "title": "The last human tasks determine the factor shares", "html": "<div class=\"columns\"><div><figure><img src=\"figures/jt_future_capital.png\" style=\"max-height:260px\" alt=\"Original Figure 5a: capital income share can tend to 100 percent, zero, or remain around 36 percent depending on whether all tasks can be automated.\"><figcaption>Capital income share (%) · calendar year<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div><div><figure><img src=\"figures/jt_future_growth.png\" style=\"max-height:260px\" alt=\"Original Figure 5b: output growth initially follows similar paths but later explodes with full automation or slows with permanent human weak links.\"><figcaption>Annual output growth (%) · calendar year<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div><\/div>\n<p>More capital → more AI research → higher productivity → more investment.<\/p>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Conditional model paths; dashed: 2% growth benchmark. <a href=\"#jt-future-details\">Output levels + faster-AI calibration<\/a><\/p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\"><span style=\"color:#8124ff\">Purple:<\/span> all tasks automated → capital share 100%; explosive growth.<br><span style=\"color:#006fb9\">Blue:<\/span> ever harder tasks → capital share stays around 36%.<br><span style=\"color:#75a826\">Green:<\/span> permanent human weak links → capital share 0%; slow growth.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5 · Figure 5a–b", "minutes": 3.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "quantitative-map", "title": "Quantitative evidence on inequality and factor shares", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:48%\"><col style=\"width:52%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Paper<\/th><th>Data and comparison<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Krusell et al. (2000)<br><em>Capital-Skill Complementarity and Inequality<\/em><\/td><td>U.S. CPS skill groups + quality-adjusted equipment; 1963–1992<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<br><em>Tasks, Automation, and the Rise in U.S. Wage Inequality<\/em><\/td><td>500 worker groups + industry automation; 1980–2016<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<br><em>AI-Augmented Capital-Skill Complementarity<\/em><\/td><td>U.S. macro data, 1963–2024; estimated AI capital model<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">Different ways to get at substitutability of labor and capital<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Krusell, Ohanian, Ríos-Rull & Violante (2000)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "quant-results-summary", "title": "Capital, tasks and skills: main results", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:30%\"><col style=\"width:70%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Paper / exhibits<\/th><th>Main result<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"#quant-korv\">Krusell et al. (2000)<\/a><\/td><td>Equipment complements skilled labour more than unskilled labour.<br>Its accumulation raises skilled workers’ relative wages.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><a href=\"#quant-ar-design\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a><br><a href=\"#quant-ar-results\">Wage & labour-share results<\/a><\/td><td>Routine-task displacement predicts lower relative wages.<br>The automation model also lowers the labour share.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><a href=\"#quant-lp-shares\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a><br><a href=\"#quant-lp-shocks\">AI shocks<\/a><\/td><td>AI complements the skilled-labour/equipment bundle <strong>less strongly<\/strong> than equipment complements skilled labour in Krusell et al.<br>Elasticities: ≈0.89 (AI–bundle) vs ≈0.67 (equipment–skill, Krusell et al.).<br>AI rental income is 1.3% of output in the estimated baseline.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Who gains depends on which tasks capital replaces and which skills it complements.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Krusell, Ohanian, Ríos-Rull & Violante (2000)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "quantitative-overview", "title": "What the quantitative results say about inequality", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:32%\"><col style=\"width:35%\"><col style=\"width:33%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Channel<\/th><th>Reported result<\/th><th>Interpretation<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Equipment + skills<br>Krusell et al.<\/td><td>≈ +20% skill premium; ≈ −40% without complementarity<\/td><td>Capital can raise wage inequality through skill demand<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Task displacement<br>Acemoglu–Restrepo<\/td><td>Full model explains ≈47% of variation in wage changes across worker groups<\/td><td>Loss of comparative-advantage tasks matters<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AI + skills<br>Luduvice–Pinheiro<\/td><td>Greater AI reliance: premium ↓<br>Cheaper AI: premium ↑<\/td><td>Model outcomes depend on AI’s production role versus its price<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Returns + ownership<br>Moll et al.<\/td><td>Income becomes more concentrated at the top<\/td><td>Asset accumulation amplifies income inequality<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">Technological change and automation have increased wage inequality.<br>Higher capital demand amplifies wealth inequality through returns and unequal ownership.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#factor-share-overview\">Capital and labour shares across papers<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Krusell, Ohanian, Ríos-Rull & Violante (2000)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · Tables 4–6", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered"}, {"title": "Euler equation and the saving rule", "html": "\n<p>At constant prices, the Euler equation gives:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\frac{\\dot c_t}{c_t}=\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\equiv b\\]<\/div>\n<p>The lifetime budget is effective wealth \\(x_t=a_t+w_z/r\\):<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[x_t=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-rs}c_t e^{bs}\\,ds=\\frac{c_t}{r-b}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[c_t=(r-b)x_t,\\qquad \\dot a_t=rx_t-c_t=bx_t\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Requires \\(r&gt;b\\); \\(b\\) is effective-wealth growth between resets. <a href=\"#moll-saving-rule\">Back to optimal saving<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 2.5, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Lemma 1; equation (3)", "id": "s09", "appendix": true, "teaching_role": "optional", "class": "content-centered"}, {"title": "Deriving the equilibrium return gap", "html": "\n<p>Set aggregate saving net of resets to zero:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[0=\\frac{r^*-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\left(K^*+\\frac{\\bar w^*}{r^*}\\right)-pK^*\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[r^*-\\rho=p\\sigma\\frac{K^*}{K^*+\\bar w^*/r^*}=p\\sigma\\underbrace{\\frac{r^*K^*}{r^*K^*+\\bar w^*}}_{\\alpha_{\\rm net}^*}\\]<\/div>\n<p>With \\(\\delta=0\\), gross and net capital shares coincide:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[r^*=\\rho+p\\sigma\\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{K^*}{Y^*}=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\rho+p\\sigma\\alpha}\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#moll-return-equilibrium\">Back to the return result<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-capital-market-equations\">Capital supply and demand<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 2.5, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equations (5) and (8)", "id": "s13", "appendix": true, "teaching_role": "optional", "revision": "definitions-2026-10-29", "class": "content-centered"}, {"id": "moll-transition", "title": "Waiting for capital accumulation need not restore wages", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:335px\" src=\"figures/results_focus_transition.png\" alt=\"Figure 3, baseline model. Black: wealth dissipation; red dashed: representative-household benchmark.\"><figcaption>Figure 3, baseline model. Black: wealth dissipation; red dashed: representative-household benchmark.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">In the baseline example, the return rises from 6.5% to about 7.2% in the long run, while the average wage remains about 1% below its initial path.<\/div><p><a href=\"#s12\" class=\"action\">Back to the main discussion<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022), Uneven Growth<\/a> · Figure 2, PDF p. 11 · Figure 3, lower panels, PDF p. 14; Section 2.4.", "minutes": 5.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "revision": "results-focus-2026-10-26", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"title": "Returns differ across assets", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:350px\" src=\"figures/asset_returns.png\" alt=\"Figure 5: alternative returns to capital and the safe rate over time.\"><figcaption>Figure 5: alternative returns to capital and the safe rate over time.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">The safe rate falls while returns associated with productive capital need not fall with it.<br>Asset composition matters for the income received by owners.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-inequality-channels\" class=\"action\">Back to inequality channels<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 4.5, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022), Uneven Growth<\/a> · Figure 5, PDF p. 23", "id": "s18", "revision": "results-2026-10-25", "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-growth-gap", "title": "The full return to investors’ wealth", "html": "\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[r_W=\\underbrace{\\kappa r_K+(1-\\kappa)r_B}_{\\text{expected portfolio return}}+\\underbrace{\\tfrac12(\\sigma-1)\\gamma\\nu^2\\kappa^2}_{\\text{risk-related saving adjustment}}\\]<\/div>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:24%\"><col style=\"width:76%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(\\gamma\\), \\(\\nu\\)<\/td><td>Risk aversion; idiosyncratic volatility of capital returns<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\kappa\\)<\/td><td>Optimal risky share of effective wealth: \\((r_K-r_B)/(\\gamma\\nu^2)\\)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(x_t\\)<\/td><td>Net assets + \\((w_z+T)/(r_B-g)\\); \\(T\\): common transfer<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<p>For \\(\\sigma&gt;1\\), capital-income risk raises saving through the income effect.<br>The empirical portfolio-return proxy omits this adjustment.<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\text{return gap}=r_W-\\rho-\\sigma g\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Figure 6 reports \\(r_W-\\sigma g\\), before subtracting constant \\(\\rho\\). <a href=\"#moll-portfolio-return\">Back to definitions<\/a> · <a href=\"#s18\">Capital and bond return evidence<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 3, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equations (13) and (17)", "revision": "definitions-2026-10-29", "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-capital-tfp", "title": "Small efficiency gains can have large distributional effects", "html": "<figure><img style=\"max-height:335px\" src=\"figures/results_focus_capital_tfp.png\" alt=\"Figure 8, capital/output and TFP panels. Changes between model steady states.\"><figcaption>Figure 8, capital/output and TFP panels. Changes between model steady states.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">TFP rises about 2% and capital relative to output about 15%.<br>These modest aggregate changes will accompany much larger differences in income growth.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-output-wages\" class=\"action\">Back to the main discussion<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 8, PDF p. 28 · Section 4.2.", "minutes": 3.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "revision": "results-focus-2026-10-26", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-tail-definition", "title": "What does a thicker upper tail mean?", "html": "<p>The tail describes how slowly the number of very high-income households declines as income rises.<\/p><div class=\"equation\">\\[\\Pr(y&gt;x)\\propto x^{-\\zeta};\\qquad \\text{figure reports }1/\\zeta\\]<\/div><table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:49%\"><col style=\"width:51%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Illustrative exponent<\/th><th>Share above 2x relative to share above x<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>ζ = 2 → 1/ζ = 0.5<\/td><td>2⁻² = 25%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>ζ = 1.5 → 1/ζ ≈ 0.67<\/td><td>2⁻¹·⁵ ≈ 35%<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">A larger 1/ζ means more mass at very high incomes.<br>It is a measure of tail inequality, not the top group’s share of national income.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-tail-mechanism\" class=\"action\">Back to the mechanism<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-tail\" class=\"action\">Back to Figure 11<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "minutes": 3, "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 11 and decomposition, PDF pp. 31–32", "revision": "definitions-2026-10-29", "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-model-data", "title": "The model captures an important pattern, with two clear gaps", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><thead><tr><th>Comparison<\/th><th>Implication<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Uneven growth across percentiles<\/td><td>Consistent with unequal exposure and concentrated capital ownership.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Labour income at the top<\/td><td>More important in the data than in the model; other skill-biased forces matter.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Speed of rising top inequality<\/td><td>The baseline wealth dynamics are too slow to match the historical transition.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">The model establishes a long-run mechanism.<br>Similar distributional patterns do not identify automation as the sole historical cause.<\/div><p><a href=\"#moll-data\" class=\"action\">Back to income data<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel and Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Sections 5.3–5.4, PDF pp. 32–35", "minutes": 6.5, "revision": "results-2026-10-25", "teaching_role": "result_interpretation", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-no-reset", "title": "The no-reset benchmark", "html": "<p>Set \\(p=0\\): households no longer lose their financial wealth in dissipation shocks.<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\frac{\\dot c}{c}=\\frac{r-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\qquad\\xrightarrow{\\text{Stationary consumption }\\dot c=0}\\qquad r^*=\\rho\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Long-run capital supply is perfectly elastic at \\(r=\\rho\\).<br>Automation changes the capital quantity; returns can rise temporarily during adjustment.<\/div>\n<p>Infinite life alone is insufficient: the key change here is removing wealth resets.<\/p>\n<p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#moll-portfolio-return\">Growth-adjusted return gap<\/a> · <a href=\"#s11\">Back to the capital-market graph<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Sections 2.1–2.2; Figure 1; footnote 15", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "optional", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "agi-worked-example", "title": "A two-task example with positive wages and a falling share", "html": "<div class=\"equation\">\\[Y=\\sqrt{y_1y_2},\\quad y_1=1+k_1/2,\\quad y_2=1+k_2/8\\]<\/div><p>Two fixed workers: one per skill. Compute costs: \\(\\gamma_1=2\\), \\(\\gamma_2=8\\).<br>When \\(K\\ge6\\), both tasks use compute; \\(A=1/8\\).<\/p><table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:18%\"><col style=\"width:25%\"><col style=\"width:32%\"><col style=\"width:25%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>K<\/th><th>Output Y<\/th><th>Total labour income<\/th><th>Labour share<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>10<\/td><td>2.50<\/td><td>1.25<\/td><td>50%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>30<\/td><td>5.00<\/td><td>1.25<\/td><td>25%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>90<\/td><td>12.50<\/td><td>1.25<\/td><td>10%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>190<\/td><td>25.00<\/td><td>1.25<\/td><td>5%<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p><a href=\"#agi-labour-share\" class=\"action\">Back to the graph<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter · illustrative special case of propositions 2–5", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-ar-scatter", "title": "Observed versus automation-predicted wage changes", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/ar_wage_scatter.png\" style=\"max-height:390px\" alt=\"Published Figure 8. Horizontal: model-predicted wage changes. Vertical: observed wage changes. Marker size: group population.\"><figcaption>Published Figure 8. Horizontal: model-predicted wage changes. Vertical: observed wage changes. Marker size: group population.<\/figcaption><\/figure><p><a href=\"#quant-ar-design\" class=\"action\">Back to task-displacement measurement<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-ar-results\" class=\"action\">Back to results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-lp-estimates", "title": "AI complementarity and three different shares", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:69%\"><col style=\"width:31%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Baseline value<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>CES production weight on AI<\/td><td>≈ 2.4%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AI share of capital value<\/td><td>2.6%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AI rental income / output<\/td><td>1.3%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AI / skilled-equipment substitution elasticity<\/td><td>≈ 0.89<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">A production weight is not an income share.<br>Gross rental income is not a return net of depreciation.<\/div><p><a href=\"#quant-lp-shares\" class=\"action\">Back to baseline shares<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "jt-setup", "title": "Jones–Tonetti: source and teaching example", "html": "<div class=\"columns\"><div><figure><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"figures/jt_paper_may.png\" style=\"max-height:325px\" alt=\"First page of Jones and Tonetti, Past Automation and Future A.I.: How Weak Links Tame the Growth Explosion, May 2026 version 0.5.\"><\/a><figcaption>Charles I. Jones & Christopher Tonetti<br>May 2026 · version 0.5<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div><div><ul><li>Two equally weighted, essential tasks<\/li><li>\\(x\\): machine task output; \\(h=1\\): human task output<\/li><li>One unit each of capital and labour; only machine productivity rises<\/li><li>Low substitution across tasks: \\(\\sigma=0.2\\)<\/li><\/ul><\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[Y=\\left(\\tfrac12x^{-4}+\\tfrac12h^{-4}\\right)^{-1/4},\\qquad s_L=\\frac{h^{-4}}{x^{-4}+h^{-4}}\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Illustrative special case; factor income = marginal product × input. <a href=\"#jt-weak-links\">Back to weak links<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5", "minutes": 1, "teaching_role": "optional_algebra", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "jt-future-details", "title": "Faster AI changes the timing; weak links still matter", "html": "<p>Left: output levels versus continued initial growth.<br>Right: growth rates; dashed 2% = historical private-business benchmark.<\/p>\n<div class=\"columns\"><div><figure><img src=\"figures/jt_future_output.png\" style=\"max-height:255px\" alt=\"Figure 5c, private-business calibration: GDP/person only4 percent above the initial trend in2050; larger divergence later.\"><figcaption>Figure 5c · private business<br>GDP per person (ratio scale; 1 = 2020)<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div><div><figure><img src=\"figures/jt_fast_growth.png\" style=\"max-height:255px\" alt=\"Figure 6b: computer-sector calibration starts output growth at4.7 percent, rising to7.3 percent in2030 and13 percent in2040, before the scenarios diverge.\"><figcaption>Figure 6b · computer-sector calibration<br>Annual output growth (%)<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">“Moore’s Law everywhere” is a conditional high-productivity scenario.<br>Its explosive paths require no permanent human weak links. <a href=\"#jt-future\">Back<\/a><\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5 · Figures 5c and 6b", "minutes": 1.5, "teaching_role": "sensitivity", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-wage-algebra", "title": "Productivity gains can accrue to capital returns", "html": "\n<p>The dual productivity identity, at competitive factor prices:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[d\\ln\\mathrm{TFP}=(1-\\alpha)\\,d\\ln\\bar w+\\alpha\\,d\\ln R\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[d\\ln\\bar w=\\frac{d\\ln\\mathrm{TFP}-\\alpha\\,d\\ln R}{1-\\alpha}\\]<\/div>\n<p>\\(R=r+\\delta\\): use the <strong>gross rental<\/strong> in this expression.<br>If \\(p=0\\), long-run \\(d\\ln R=0\\); productivity gains raise mean wages.<\/p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">With upward-sloping capital supply, \\(R\\) can rise permanently.<br>Workers receive less of the productivity gain; mean wages can fall.<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Local changes; fixed skill supplies. <a href=\"#moll-wage-equation\">Back to wage levels<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equation (10); footnote 16", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-risk-tail", "title": "The income-tail equation with growth and return risk", "html": "\n<p>Scale effective wealth by trend growth. Define:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[b=\\frac{r_W-\\rho}{\\sigma}-g,\\qquad v=\\kappa\\nu\\]<\/div>\n<p>\\(b\\): relative accumulation drift; \\(v\\): portfolio-return volatility.<br>The stationary Pareto exponent \\(\\zeta\\) satisfies:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[p=\\zeta b+\\tfrac12\\zeta(\\zeta-1)v^2\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\frac1\\zeta=\\frac{b-\\tfrac12v^2+\\sqrt{(b-\\tfrac12v^2)^2+2pv^2}}{2p}\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">No-risk case, \\(b&gt;0\\): \\(1/\\zeta=b/p\\). Equation (15), written compactly. <a href=\"#moll-tail\">Back to Figure 11<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Proposition 4; equation (15)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-tail-mechanism", "title": "From faster accumulation to a thicker income tail", "html": "\n<p><strong>Baseline:<\/strong> one asset, no trend growth; time \\(T\\) since the last reset.<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[x(T)=x_0e^{(r^*-\\rho)T/\\sigma},\\qquad \\Pr(T&gt;t)=e^{-pt}\\]<\/div>\n<p>Some households accumulate uninterruptedly for much longer than others.<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\underbrace{\\frac1\\zeta}_{\\text{tail index}}=\\frac{\\text{accumulation rate}}{\\text{reset rate}}=\\frac{r^*-\\rho}{\\sigma p}=\\alpha_{\\rm net}^*\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Illustration: accumulation 2% / reset rate 4% → tail index 0.5.<br>Faster accumulation at 2.4% → index 0.6; more mass at high incomes.<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">\\(\\zeta\\): Pareto exponent; \\(x_0=w_z/r^*\\). <a href=\"#moll-tail-definition\">Tail probabilities<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-risk-tail\">Risky-model extension<\/a><\/p><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#moll-inequality-channels\">Back to inequality channels<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Proposition 3; Figure 4", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-portfolio-return", "title": "Portfolio returns and the growth-adjusted return gap", "html": "\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:24%\"><col style=\"width:76%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Meaning in the extended model<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(r_K\\), \\(r_B\\)<\/td><td>After-tax real return: productive capital / safe bonds<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>\\(\\kappa\\)<\/td><td>Risky capital / effective wealth; \\(\\kappa&gt;1\\) means borrowing<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[r_W=\\underbrace{\\kappa r_K+(1-\\kappa)r_B}_{\\text{portfolio return}}+\\text{risk adjustment}\\]<\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\">\\[\\underbrace{r_W-\\rho-\\sigma g}_{\\text{return gap}}=\\sigma\\left(\\frac{r_W-\\rho}{\\sigma}-g\\right)\\]<\/div>\n<p class=\"table-note\">\\(g\\): trend growth. <a href=\"#moll-growth-gap\">Full risky-return formula<\/a> · <a href=\"#s18\">Capital and bond evidence<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-inequality-channels\">Back to inequality channels<\/a><\/p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">A higher return gap means faster accumulation relative to the growing economy.<\/div>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · equations (13) and (17)", "minutes": 3, "teaching_role": "setup", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "moll-tail", "title": "Higher returns thicken the upper income tail", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/moll_tail.png\" style=\"max-height:305px\" alt=\"Figure 11: upper income-tail inequality and its return and portfolio-risk components.\"><figcaption>Figure 11 · extended model: return gap + portfolio risk. <a href=\"#moll-tail-definition\">Index definition<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-risk-tail\">Tail equation<\/a><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">Income-tail index: 0.54 → 0.65.<br>Higher returns alone account for the increase to about 0.63.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#s22\">Back to model income growth<\/a> · <a href=\"#moll-inequality-channels\">Back to inequality channels<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19417\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moll, Rachel & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Figure 11", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "agi-wage-bill", "title": "A falling labour share need not mean lower total wages", "html": "<ul><li>In the core model, the aggregate wage bill stays at least as high as in the no-AGI economy<\/li><li>Human skills can still produce; the old production method remains available<\/li><li>Some workers can nevertheless suffer wage losses during the transition<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"takeaway\">This result needs efficient markets, constant returns and only two scarce factors:<br>human skills and compute. Other fixed resources can change the comparison.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#agi-labour-share\">Back to the main AGI result<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "jt-historical", "title": "Freezing automation would remove much of past TFP growth", "html": "<p><strong>Data:<\/strong> BEA/BLS accounts + LLM reconstruction of automated-task labour costs.<br><strong>Counterfactual:<\/strong> freeze automation; keep task-productivity growth unchanged.<\/p>\n<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:34%\"><col style=\"width:22%\"><col style=\"width:23%\"><col style=\"width:21%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Sector / period<\/th><th>Actual TFP growth<br>(% / year)<\/th><th>TFP growth lost<br>(pp / year)<\/th><th>Lost / actual growth<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Private business (1950–2023)<\/td><td>1.2<\/td><td>1.3<\/td><td>108%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Computers (1987–2017)<\/td><td>12.8<\/td><td>3.2<\/td><td>25%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Retail trade (1987–2017)<\/td><td>1.7<\/td><td>4.1<\/td><td>242%<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table>\n<p class=\"table-note\">Model accounting, Table 7: averages across subperiods; shares use unrounded calculations. <a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paper: full table + assumptions<\/a><\/p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Machines improve fastest: automation moves more tasks onto that growth path.<br>Contributions can exceed 100% when other forces offset automation’s gains.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#jt-weak-links\">Back to weak links<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5 · Table 7; Sections 2–4", "minutes": 3.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-korv", "title": "Equipment complements skilled labour", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/korv_skill_premium.png\" style=\"max-height:265px\" alt=\"Skill premium = college-graduate wage / non-graduate wage. Reported changes, 1963–1992.\"><figcaption>Skill premium = college-graduate wage / non-graduate wage. Reported changes, 1963–1992.<\/figcaption><\/figure><table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:73%\"><col style=\"width:27%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Equipment substitution elasticity<\/th><th>Estimate<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>With unskilled labour<\/td><td>1.67<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>With skilled labour<\/td><td>0.67<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paper: original estimates<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-results-summary\">Back to main results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0262.00150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Krusell, Ohanian, Ríos-Rull & Violante (2000)<\/a>", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-ar-design", "title": "Task displacement predicts relative wage declines", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:32%\"><col style=\"width:68%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Object<\/th><th>Construction<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Observation<\/td><td>500 groups: education, sex, age, race & nativity<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Task displacement<\/td><td>Initial industry/task specialization × subsequent automation-related task losses<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Wage outcome<\/td><td>Change in real hourly wages, 1980–2016<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Direct wage relationship<\/td><td>Task displacement accounts for 50–70% of variation in wage changes<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">Routine-task workers lose relative pay in rapidly automating industries.<br>The full model adds productivity, task reallocation and industry-composition effects.<\/div><p><a href=\"#quant-ar-scatter\" class=\"action\">Observed versus model-predicted wages<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-results-summary\">Back to main results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Sections 3–5", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-ar-results", "title": "Large wage inequality effects can accompany modest efficiency gains", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:55%\"><col style=\"width:22.5%\"><col style=\"width:22.5%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Outcome, 1980–2016<\/th><th>U.S. data<\/th><th>Automation model<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>College wage premium: 100 × Δlog<\/td><td>25.51<\/td><td>21.02<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Population in groups with declining wages<\/td><td>53.10%<\/td><td>42.26%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TFP: 100 × Δlog<\/td><td>35.00<\/td><td>3.42<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Labour share change<\/td><td>−8.00 pp<\/td><td>−10.41 pp<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">The full model accounts for about 47% of wage-structure changes.<br>Automation has large distributional effects, with limited TFP gains.<br>The model’s labour-share decline exceeds the observed drop.<\/div><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paper: original Table VIII<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-results-summary\">Back to main results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a> · Table VIII, columns 1 and 3", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-lp-shares", "title": "AI capital is a small part of the estimated baseline", "html": "<figure><img src=\"figures/lp_baseline_income_shares.png\" style=\"max-height:315px\" alt=\"Luduvice–Pinheiro, Table 4. Calibrated baseline shares of output; capital rental income includes depreciation.\"><figcaption>Luduvice–Pinheiro, Table 4. Calibrated baseline shares of output; capital rental income includes depreciation.<\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"takeaway\">AI is 2.6% of capital value and receives 1.3% of output as rental income.<br>This baseline is far from the all-work AGI limit.<\/div><p><a href=\"#quant-lp-estimates\" class=\"action\">Estimated complementarity and original table<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-results-summary\">Back to main results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "quant-lp-shocks", "title": "More AI use and cheaper AI are different shocks", "html": "<p><strong>Estimated complementarity:<\/strong> AI and the skilled-labour/equipment composite have substitution elasticity ≈ 0.89.<\/p><table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:48%\"><col style=\"width:26%\"><col style=\"width:26%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>New steady state<\/th><th>Double AI weight<\/th><th>Halve AI price<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Output change<\/td><td>−1.87%<\/td><td>+1.00%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>AI capital stock change<\/td><td>+81%<\/td><td>+89%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Skilled/unskilled wage ratio<\/td><td>1.73<\/td><td>1.85<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><p>Initial wage ratio: 1.82. AI production weight: 2.4% → 4.8% in the first experiment.<\/p><div class=\"takeaway\">A higher weight puts more demand on scarce complementary AI.<br>Cheaper AI relaxes that constraint and raises the skill premium.<\/div><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paper: counterfactual tables<\/a> · <a href=\"#quant-results-summary\">Back to main results<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "result_exhibit", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}, {"id": "factor-share-overview", "title": "What the results say about capital and labour shares", "html": "<table class=\"results\"><colgroup><col style=\"width:27%\"><col style=\"width:38%\"><col style=\"width:35%\"><\/colgroup><thead><tr><th>Exercise<\/th><th>Labour / capital result<\/th><th>Interpretation<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Task displacement<br>Acemoglu–Restrepo<\/td><td>Labour share: −8 pp in data;<br>−10.41 pp in model<\/td><td>Large share shifts;<br>modest TFP gains<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Estimated AI baseline<br>Luduvice–Pinheiro<\/td><td>Labour: 68.4%;<br>AI rental income: 1.3%<\/td><td>Current AI is small<br>in this model<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Weak-link scenarios<br>Jones–Tonetti<\/td><td>Capital share can rise,<br>fall or stay stable<\/td><td>Remaining human tasks<br>can retain scarcity value<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Conditional AGI limit<br>Restrepo<\/td><td>Labour share → 0;<br>compute share → 1<\/td><td>Positive wages can persist;<br>ownership becomes central<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><div class=\"takeaway\">A smaller labour share does not by itself imply lower wages.<br>Combine productivity, wage levels and asset ownership to assess living standards.<\/div><p class=\"table-note\"><a href=\"#quantitative-overview\">Back to inequality<\/a><\/p>", "notes": "", "source": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3982/ECTA19815\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Acemoglu & Restrepo (2022)<\/a>; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-wp-202622\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luduvice & Pinheiro (2026)<\/a> · August text / September WP 26-22; <a href=\"https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/c/4765/files/2025/10/AGI_v4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Restrepo (2025)<\/a> · October 22 chapter; <a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/JonesTonetti_Automation.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones & Tonetti (2026)<\/a> · May v0.5", "minutes": 2.5, "teaching_role": "interpretation", "class": "content-centered", "appendix": true}], "revision_date": "2026-10-31", "revision_focus": "Graph-led long-run models; explicit actors, choices, definitions, units and limiting assumptions; optional derivations linked in appendix; quantitative inequality and factor-share overview. Jones–Tonetti May v0.5: weak links, historical accounting and alternative factor-share paths. Uneven Growth mathematical exposition: production, optimal saving, capital clearing, wage levels, portfolio returns and tail equations.", "pacing": {"main_slides": 36, "appendix_slides": 27, "guidance": "Aim for roughly 70% results and interpretation / 30% question and setup in delivery. Do not omit necessary definitions to hit an exact split; slide timings remain rough preparation aids.", "note": "Expanded instructor-review draft. The original 110-minute slot is planning metadata; choose optional figures and appendix details after review."}, "content_minutes": 111.0, "hideNotes": true};
